---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: doorway-service-src-seam-birth-rule
purpose: >
  Nested under `../.epr-meta` (`covers: subtree`) — this manifest does not reclaim
  coverage; it adds two edit-time signals. The first is plan task P4.5, the seam-concern
  contract architecture's design-time birth rule. `doorway/doorway-service/
  seam-registry.yaml` already registers this crate's decision surface
  (decide_reconcile/ReconcileDecision, classify_dispatch/Disposition,
  epr_should_serve_ssr/SsrFallbackReason, fetch_projections_with_fallback/
  FallbackOutcome, K2Store::put_at/K2PutOutcome, should_serve_response) — the
  rule below catches the NEXT one before it ships unregistered. The second,
  `web-seam-placement` (2026-10-06), sends a new source file born here
  to a background placement review against the hub seam.
rules:
  - id: seam-birth-rule
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["Decision {", "Verdict {", "Outcome {", "Reason {",
                     "Disposition {", "fn decide_", "Answer<", ".route(",
                     "match (method"]
    dedupe-of: ".claude/skills/p2p-design-gate/SKILL.md (Step 4: Concern-Canon Answer) + doorway/doorway-service/seam-registry.yaml (this crate's decision-point registry) — where a new decision surface answers the canon and gets registered"
    why: >
      This shape (a verdict/decision/outcome/reason enum, a `decide_*` predicate,
      an `Answer<T>` boundary type, or a route registration) is exactly what the
      seam-concern contract architecture's birth rule governs: a NEW decision
      predicate, boundary answer type, sync message, or route may not be born
      unregistered. Before shipping one: walk `p2p-design-gate` Step 4 — answer
      C0-C14 (`answered`/`partial`/`unbound`/`n-a`-with-justification) against the
      guarantee in its canon home (predicate-bearing classes are enforcement rows
      in `.claude/epr-meta/policies.yaml`, currently C2/C6a; the rest are
      Precedent-shaped rows in `.claude/epr-meta/concerns.yaml`) — then add a row
      to `doorway/doorway-service/seam-registry.yaml` (schema:
      `elohim/sdk/schemas/v1/manifest/seam-registry.schema.json`). The census
      (`placement-audit.py --epr-meta`) is the enforcement backstop, not this
      advisory: it fails loud on an unregistered point, an uncited contract test,
      or a mirrored test. This rule matches file CONTENT, not added lines, so an
      edit anywhere in an already-registered file (`http.rs`, `render/registry.rs`,
      `projection/epr_router.rs`, `bootstrap/k2.rs`, `cache/reach_aware_serving.rs`)
      re-fires too — the accepted cost of a declarative advisory that never
      blocks (never a validator: this is a judgement call, not a textual proof).
  - id: web-seam-placement
    class: dispatch
    when: { write: "*.rs", new: true }
    route-to: { dest: elohim/elohim-hub/ }
    parameters:
      dispatch-agent: organizer
      dispatch-prompt: >
        A new Rust source file is being born in doorway-service. Apply the placement test in
        elohim/elohim-hub/README.md, section "The web seam" (that README is the statement of the
        test; this prompt only summarises it): does this concern exist only because
        the other end cannot address and verify a peer by key? Report exactly one of KEEP (it
        serves a browser, a crawler or a web2 protocol: a registered name, the certificate for it,
        TLS termination, host-to-app routing, a cache for traffic with no relationship, a web2
        protocol projection; say which), ROUTE (it is web-level but works when the peer is reached
        by its key: name elohim/elohim-hub, whose crate the first new piece creates; or it is
        about bytes, heads, custody or reach: name elohim-storage)
        or SPLIT (name the half that stays and the half that goes), with the shortest supporting
        reason. Do not create, move or rewrite files; report to the editing agent and let
        development continue.
    why: >
      doorway-service is for what exists only because the other end cannot address and verify a
      peer by key (a browser, a crawler, a web2 protocol). Web-level work a peer needs
      when it is reached by key (serving an app's files, rendering a page, judging which head is
      deliverable) belongs in elohim/elohim-hub/, so the same code serves a person's own device, a
      household hub and the peer behind a doorway. New hub-side work starts that crate; it does
      not deepen the debt here. About seven thousand lines of hub-side work grew in
      this crate (`render/{registry,capability,breaker,bundle_heads,coherence}.rs`, `ssr.rs`,
      `routes/chrome.rs`) before the seam was marked on 2026-10-06; `render/warm_shell.rs` and
      `routes/apps.rs` are edge caches and belong here. The list is in the hub README.
      Birth-time only: editing an existing file never fires this.
    retire-when: >
      when the dependency graph is the boundary: doorway-service no longer depends on elohim-render,
      and a renderer or a head judgement cannot compile in this crate
cites:
  - seam-concern-contract-architecture-plan
---

# doorway/doorway-service/src — seam birth rule (concern canon)

`../CLAUDE.md` and this crate's `seam-registry.yaml` describe the decision surface
itself; this manifest only adds the edit-time reminder that a *new* one (plan task
P4.5) must answer the concern canon and register before it ships. See
`.claude/epr-meta/policies.yaml` + `.claude/epr-meta/concerns.yaml` for the sixteen
classes (C0-C14, C6 split C6a/C6b) and the `p2p-design-gate` skill's Step 4 for the
per-class checklist.
